In this lesson, we'll look at three key ideas that describe any function: the domain, the co-domain, and the range. Think of the domain as the set of allowed inputs, the x-values you are actually allowed to plug in. The co-domain is the bigger set that the outputs are said to live in, often just the real numbers. And the range is the set of outputs the function actually achieves. So the range always sits inside the co-domain, like a smaller subset.

Now, when a question gives you a rule without spelling out the domain, we go looking for the maximal domain, meaning the biggest set of x-values where the rule still gives you a real number. Two big things to watch for. First, division by zero is not allowed, so any denominator that can become zero gives you a value to exclude. For example, one over the quantity x minus two cannot have x equal to two. Second, you cannot take the square root of a negative and stay in the real numbers, so whatever sits under a square root has to be greater than or equal to zero. You will usually write these domains using interval notation, where a square bracket means the endpoint is included and a round bracket means it is not, and infinity always wears a round bracket. Now let's see it in action.